#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100538 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d68c23-6a62-538d-adf1-3a20a474f107" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center of a Circle on the Coordinate Plane Using the Midpoint Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "fd6cab66-924e-5fbf-8800-53b10e1e6623" :question/math-academy-id "e-3207" :question/problem "Find the coordinates $(h,k)$ of the center of the circle above given that the points $(2,-3)$ and $(10,5)$ lie on a diameter of the circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1183/Source/Images/eab58ac1804896bc3b18dcaac02635ff.png)" :question/worked-solution "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. Therefore, we can calculate it using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (h,k) &= \\left(\\dfrac{x_1+x_2}{2}, \\dfrac {y_1+y_2}{2}\\right) \\\\[2pt] &= \\left(\\dfrac{10+2}2,\\dfrac{5+(-3)}2\\right) \\\\[2pt] &= \\left(\\dfrac{12}2,\\dfrac{2}2\\right) \\\\[2pt] &= (6, 1). \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100539 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e9df1ae-8176-555b-987d-94773c27d435" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of a Circle on the Coordinate Plane Using the Distance Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "f091473d-0317-5c94-999f-4626dd93d021" :question/math-academy-id "e-3208" :question/problem "The points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(-5,3)$ and $(-3,7)$, respectively, and the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle. Find the radius of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The diameter can be calculated using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\sqrt{(x_{1} - x_{2})^{2} + (y_{1} - y_{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-5 - (-3))^{2} + (3 - 7)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-2)^{2} + (-4)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 + 16} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{20} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is half the diameter:\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{d}{2} = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{5}}{2} = \\sqrt{5}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100540 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52384502-66c5-5089-b38f-d46b94877430" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Radius of a Circle on the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "4441ee7d-39ac-5aaf-ae23-aea9e9eb7ec7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3209" :question/problem "The segment $\\overline{CD}$ is the diameter of a circle where the points $C$ and $D$ have coordinates $(7a,6a)$ and $(a,-2a)$, respectively, with $a>0$. Find the coordinates of the center and the radius of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. So, we can calculate the coordinates of the center using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(h, k) &= (\\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2}, \\frac{y_{1} + y_{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7a + a}{2}, (6a + (-2a))/(2)) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{8a}{2}, \\frac{4a}{2}) \\\\\n&= (4a, 2a)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius can be found by calculating the distance between the center $(4a, 2a)$ and a point on the circle, such as $C(7a,6a)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\sqrt{(x_{1} - x_{2})^{2} + (y_{1} - y_{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(4a - 7a)^{2} + (2a - 6a)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-3a)^{2} + (-4a)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9a^{2} + 16a^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25a^{2}} \\\\\n&= 5 \\mid a \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $a>0$, we have that\n\n$$\nr = 5\\vert a \\vert = 5a\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center of the circle is at $(4a, 2a)$ and the radius is $5a$."}]}
